29	/**
30	 Safelists define what HTML (elements and attributes) to allow through a {@link Cleaner}. Everything else is removed.
31	 <p>
32	 Start with one of the defaults:
33	 </p>
34	 <ul>
35	 <li>{@link #none}
36	 <li>{@link #simpleText}
37	 <li>{@link #basic}
38	 <li>{@link #basicWithImages}
39	 <li>{@link #relaxed}
40	 </ul>
41	 <p>
42	 If you need to allow more through (please be careful!), tweak a base safelist with:
43	 </p>
44	 <ul>
45	 <li>{@link #addTags(String... tagNames)}
46	 <li>{@link #addAttributes(String tagName, String... attributes)}
47	 <li>{@link #addEnforcedAttribute(String tagName, String attribute, String value)}
48	 <li>{@link #addProtocols(String tagName, String attribute, String... protocols)}
49	 </ul>
50	 <p>
51	 You can remove any setting from an existing safelist with:
52	 </p>
53	 <ul>
54	 <li>{@link #removeTags(String... tagNames)}
55	 <li>{@link #removeAttributes(String tagName, String... attributes)}
56	 <li>{@link #removeEnforcedAttribute(String tagName, String attribute)}
57	 <li>{@link #removeProtocols(String tagName, String attribute, String... removeProtocols)}
58	 </ul>
59	
60	 <p>
61	 The {@link Cleaner} and these safelists assume that you want to clean a <code>body</code> fragment of HTML (to add user
62	 supplied HTML into a templated page), and not to clean a full HTML document. If the latter is the case, you could wrap
63	 the templated document HTML around the cleaned body HTML.
64	 </p>
65	 <p>
66	 Safelists are mutable. A {@link Cleaner} uses the supplied safelist directly, so later changes affect later cleaning
67	 calls. If you want to share a safelist across threads, finish configuring it first and do not mutate it while it is in
68	 use. To build a variant from an existing configuration, use {@link #Safelist(Safelist)} to make a copy.
69	 </p>
70	 <p>
71	 If you are going to extend a safelist, please be very careful. Make sure you understand what attributes may lead to
72	 XSS attack vectors. URL attributes are particularly vulnerable and require careful validation. See 
73	 the <a href="https://owasp.org/www-community/xss-filter-evasion-cheatsheet">XSS Filter Evasion Cheat Sheet</a> for some
74	 XSS attack examples (that jsoup will safeguard against with the default Cleaner and Safelist configuration).
75	 </p>
76	 */
77	public class Safelist {
78	    private static final String All = ":all";
79	    private static final TagName AllTag = TagName.valueOf(All);
80	    private static final String Srcset = "srcset";
81	    private static final AttributeKey SrcsetKey = AttributeKey.valueOf(Srcset);
82	    private static final String Style = "style";
83	    private static final AttributeKey StyleKey = AttributeKey.valueOf(Style);
84	    // external references in an allowed inline style attribute default to the same safe URL protocols as an image:
85	    // http(s), plus same-document fragment references such as url(#gradient). Any other protocol (javascript:,
86	    // vbscript:, data:, or an unknown scheme) is rejected unless the caller configures an explicit protocol set for
87	    // the tag's style attribute.
88	    private static final Set<Protocol> DefaultStyleProtocols = Collections.unmodifiableSet(new HashSet<>(Arrays.asList(
89	        Protocol.valueOf("http"), Protocol.valueOf("https"), Protocol.valueOf("#"))));
90	    private final Set<TagName> tagNames; // tags allowed, lower case. e.g. [p, br, span]
91	    private final Map<TagName, Set<AttributeKey>> attributes; // tag -> attribute[]. allowed attributes [href] for a tag.
92	    private final Map<TagName, Map<AttributeKey, AttributeValue>> enforcedAttributes; // always set these attribute values
93	    private final Map<TagName, Map<AttributeKey, Set<Protocol>>> protocols; // allowed URL protocols for attributes
94	    private boolean preserveRelativeLinks; // option to preserve relative links
